\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 The Physical System
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We consider 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}$
\end_inset

 spin-1/2 fermions moving in one dimension.
 The Hamiltonian (in atomic units,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hbar=m=1$
\end_inset

) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{H}=\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}\left[-\frac{1}{2}\frac{d^{2}}{dx_{i}^{2}}+V_{\text{ext}}(x_{i})\right]+\sum_{1\le i<j\le N_{\text{el}}}v_{\text{ee}}(x_{i}-x_{j}),
\]

\end_inset

where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\text{ext}}(x)=\frac{1}{2}\omega^{2}x^{2}$
\end_inset

 (harmonic trap),
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{\text{ee}}(r)=1/\sqrt{r^{2}+a_{\text{soft}}^{2}}$
\end_inset

 (soft-Coulomb interaction).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The total wavefunction 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(x_{1},\sigma_{1},\dots,x_{N_{\text{el}}},\sigma_{N_{\text{el}}})$
\end_inset

 must be antisymmetric under exchange of any two particles (including their spins).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Discretization on a Spatial Grid
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We restrict the spatial domain to 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in[-L,L]$
\end_inset

 and discretize it into 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 equally spaced points:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{j}=-L+(j-1)\Delta x,\qquad\Delta x=\frac{2L}{N-1},\qquad j=1,\dots,N.
\]

\end_inset

The single-particle Hamiltonian (kinetic + external) is represented as an 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times N$
\end_inset

 matrix in this grid basis.
 The kinetic term 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{1}{2}\dfrac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}$
\end_inset

 is approximated by a finite-difference stencil of order 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(-\frac{1}{2}\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}\psi\right)(x_{j})\approx-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=-s}^{s}c_{k}\frac{\psi(x_{j+k})}{\Delta x^{2}},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s=(m-1)/2$
\end_inset

 and the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{k}$
\end_inset

 are standard finite-difference weights for the second derivative (e.g.,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $m=3$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{-1}=1,c_{0}=-2,c_{1}=1$
\end_inset

).
 This yields a sparse symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 with elements
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{j,j+k}=-\frac{1}{2}\frac{c_{k}}{\Delta x^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The external potential is diagonal:
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{j,j}=V_{\text{ext}}(x_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus the single-particle matrix is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{h}=\mathbf{T}+\mathbf{V}_{\text{ext}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Many-Body Hamiltonian in the Product Basis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For distinguishable particles,
 we take the basis states as simple products of single-particle grid states:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|i_{1},i_{2},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}}\rangle=|i_{1}\rangle\otimes|i_{2}\rangle\otimes\cdots\otimes|i_{N_{\text{el}}}\rangle,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where each 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{p}$
\end_inset

 runs from 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
 The corresponding wavefunction is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{i_{1},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}}}(x_{1},\dots,x_{N_{\text{el}}})=\prod_{p=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}\delta_{x_{p},x_{i_{p}}}
\]

\end_inset

This basis has dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{N_{\text{el}}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.1 Non-interacting part
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The single-particle operator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{p=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}\left(-\frac{1}{2}\frac{d^{2}}{dx_{p}^{2}}+V_{\text{ext}}(x_{p})\right)
\]

\end_inset

 acts on each particle independently.
 In the product basis,
 it becomes a sum of Kronecker products:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{H}_{0}=\sum_{p=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}\underbrace{\mathbf{I}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{h}}_{\text{position }p}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{I}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we order the basis such that the first particle index varies fastest (column-major order),
 the linear index corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $(i_{1},i_{2},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{idx}=1+\sum_{p=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}(i_{p}-1)N^{p-1}.
\]

\end_inset

Then the term for particle 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 can be constructed by taking the Kronecker product in 
\series bold
reverse order
\series default
:
 start with the factor for particle 1,
 then multiply from the left by the factor for particle 2,
 etc.
 The Julia function build_term(p) implements this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.2 Interaction part
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two-body interaction 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i<j}v_{\text{ee}}(x_{i}-x_{j})$
\end_inset

 is diagonal in the product basis because the basis states are localized at grid points.
 Its matrix elements are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\langle i_{1},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}}|\hat{V}_{\text{int}}|j_{1},\dots,j_{N_{\text{el}}}\rangle=\delta_{i_{1},j_{1}}\cdots\delta_{i_{N_{\text{el}}},j_{N_{\text{el}}}}\sum_{p<q}v_{\text{ee}}(x_{i_{p}}-x_{i_{q}}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus the interaction contributes a diagonal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{U}$
\end_inset

 with entries
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{\text{idx}}=\sum_{1\le p<q\le N_{\text{el}}}v_{\text{ee}}(x_{i_{p}}-x_{i_{q}}).
\]

\end_inset

The total many-body Hamiltonian in the product basis is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{H}=\mathbf{H}_{0}+\mathbf{U}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.
 Exact Diagonalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use ARPACK to compute the lowest few eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the sparse matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{H}$
\end_inset

 (real,
 symmetric).
 For a given number of computed states 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain eigenvalues 
\begin_inset Formula $E^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 and corresponding eigenvectors 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{c}^{(n)}$
\end_inset

 in the product basis.
 Each eigenvector can be reshaped into a tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi^{(n)}(i_{1},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}})$
\end_inset

 of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times\cdots\times N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.
 Antisymmetrization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The eigenvectors from the product basis correspond to distinguishable particles.
 To obtain physical fermionic states,
 we must antisymmetrize the wavefunction including spin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.1 Spin part
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a given spin configuration specified by the string `electrons` (e.g.,
 `"uud"`),
 we construct the spin tensor as a product of spinors:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\chi(\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{N_{\text{el}}})=\bigotimes_{p=1}^{N_{\text{el}}}\begin{cases}
\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix} & \text{if spin up},\\
\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix} & \text{if spin down}.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This yields a tensor of shape 
\begin_inset Formula $(2,2,\dots,2)$
\end_inset

 (dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{N_{\text{el}}}$
\end_inset

) with a single non-zero entry at the index corresponding to the given spin configuration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.2 Full wavefunction (spatial + spin)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We combine spatial and spin degrees of freedom into a single tensor with alternating dimensions:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi(x_{1},\sigma_{1},x_{2},\sigma_{2},\dots,x_{N_{\text{el}}},\sigma_{N_{\text{el}}})=\Psi(x_{1},\dots,x_{N_{\text{el}}})\cdot\chi(\sigma_{1},\dots,\sigma_{N_{\text{el}}}).
\]

\end_inset

In the discretized representation,
 this is an array of size 
\begin_inset Formula $(N,2,N,2,\dots,N,2)$
\end_inset

.
 The element at indices 
\begin_inset Formula $(i_{1},s_{1},i_{2},s_{2},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}},s_{N_{\text{el}}})$
\end_inset

 equals 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi(i_{1},\dots,i_{N_{\text{el}}})\cdot\chi(s_{1},\dots,s_{N_{\text{el}}})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.3 Permutation sum
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To enforce antisymmetry,
 we sum over all permutations 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}$
\end_inset

 particles,
 each weighted by the sign of the permutation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi_{\text{anti}}=\sum_{P}\mathrm{sgn}(P)\;\Phi^{(P)},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{(P)}$
\end_inset

 is the wavefunction with the coordinates of particle 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 replaced by those of particle 
\begin_inset Formula $P(p)$
\end_inset

.
 In tensor terms,
 we permute the particle dimensions accordingly.
 For example,
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}=2$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi_{\text{anti}}(x_{1},\sigma_{1},x_{2},\sigma_{2})=\Phi(x_{1},\sigma_{1},x_{2},\sigma_{2})-\Phi(x_{2},\sigma_{2},x_{1},\sigma_{1}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sign 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{sgn}(P)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 for even permutations,
 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 for odd ones.
 In the implementation,
 we loop over all permutations,
 compute the required dimension permutation using `permutedims`,
 and accumulate the result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
5.4 Normalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After summation,
 we normalize:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Phi_{\text{anti}}\leftarrow\frac{\Phi_{\text{anti}}}{\sqrt{\sum_{\text{all indices}}|\Phi_{\text{anti}}|^{2}\;(\Delta x)^{N_{\text{el}}}}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the factor 
\begin_inset Formula $(\Delta x)^{N_{\text{el}}}$
\end_inset

 accounts for the volume element in the continuous inner product.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
6.
 Final States
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The antisymmetrized states 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{\text{anti}}$
\end_inset

 now represent proper fermionic wavefunctions.
 Duplicate states (identical up to a global phase) are removed.
 The ground state (or any desired state) is extracted,
 and its energy is the corresponding eigenvalue from the diagonalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
7.
 Remarks on the Implementation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Memory and performance:
 The dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{N_{\text{el}}}$
\end_inset

 grows quickly.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $N=60$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}=2$
\end_inset

 it is 3600;
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $N=40$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}=3$
\end_inset

 it is 64,000.
 Sparse matrices and ARPACK make such sizes feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Basis ordering:
 The code carefully uses column-major indexing (particle 1 varies fastest) to match the `reshape` and `LinearIndices` conventions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Antisymmetrization loop:
 The permutation loop uses `permutedims` with a `Tuple` of the new dimension order.
 This is efficient for small 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}$
\end_inset

 (the number of permutations is 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}!$
\end_inset

,
 which is manageable for 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{\text{el}}\le4$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Spin:
 The spin part is constructed as a tensor product of spinors.
 For configurations with more than one electron of the same spin,
 the resulting spin tensor may have multiple non-zero entries,
 but the antisymmetrization automatically mixes them to yield the correct spin multiplets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This approach directly implements the many-body Schrödinger equation on a real-space grid,
 without approximating the interaction by a basis of non-interacting orbitals.
 It is conceptually straightforward and yields exact (up to discretization) results for the given grid.
\end_layout

